{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-21 18:28:04 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 01:28:04 +07  \nCoding-Agent: codex  \nSession-ID: 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a\n\n## Architecture ruling\n\nI recommend architecture B: generate a lossless, invertible de Bruijn\nexecutable encoding from the named `:yin/*` datoms. The projection is\nintentionally lossy and remains the alpha-equivalence identity layer. It\ncannot be the executable source while preserving exact scalar spelling,\nfront-end tail flags, binder names, provenance, and named runtime behavior.\n\nThe executable image therefore has its own code-image hash and cache key. The\nprojection fingerprint may be attached as metadata or used to find\nalpha-equivalent candidates, but equal projection fingerprints may legitimately\nproduce different executable images. The lowerer reuses the existing D1 scope\nresolver without modifying the projection.\n\n## Finding-by-finding disposition\n\n```text\nFinding   Disposition   Revised treatment\nP1-1      ACCEPTED      Exact named spelling is retained; image hash, not\n                       projection fingerprint, keys executable caches.\nP1-2      ACCEPTED      Front-end :yin/tail? is copied; syntax is not inferred.\nP1-3      ACCEPTED      Free environment is fixed initial VM state; named\n                       register leakage is a separate defect or B5 refusal.\nP1-4      ACCEPTED      B0 defines normalized comparison for closures,\n                       continuations, parked records, streams, stores, errors.\nP1-5      ACCEPTED      B2 and B1 both validate scope; malformed images cannot\n                       bypass validation at load.\n\nP2-1      ACCEPTED      Positional nil-fill/drop-extra binding is specified;\n                       named bind-params remains unchanged.\nP2-2      ACCEPTED      Only application carries tail?; ffi-call does not.\nP2-3      ACCEPTED      New opcodes mirror vector-operand-table exactly.\nP2-4      ACCEPTED      completion.cljc is named-VM-only; a de Bruijn adapter\n                       is future work.\nP2-5      ACCEPTED      load-vector alias wording now distinguishes segment-key\n                       from :yin.code/hash.\nP2-6      ACCEPTED      B5 names parity, content, and completion corpora rather\n                       than treating helper tests as execution fixtures.\nP2-7      ACCEPTED      Error message and normalized ex-data are compared.\nP2-8      ACCEPTED      Only data-oriented engine helpers are reused; named\n                       telemetry and wait-entry helpers are reimplemented.\nP2-9      ACCEPTED      Occurrence expansion is bounded by named linearization;\n                       future lambda sharing requires a specified convention.\nP2-10     ACCEPTED      Memo context is explicitly the vector of frame arities\n                       plus node identity and tail context.\n\nP3-1      ACCEPTED      Operational model-routing guidance was removed.\nP3-2      ACCEPTED      Encoder reuse of debruijn value encoding, descriptor\n                       hashing, and Jing hashing is stated.\nP3-3      ACCEPTED      :macro? is retained losslessly but ignored at runtime.\nP3-4      ACCEPTED      IVMState/environment returns fixed free-env; frames are\n                       machine state.\nP3-5      ACCEPTED      Frame order and innermost indexing are explicit.\nP3-6      ACCEPTED      B0 no longer lists the existing design file as new.\nP3-7      ACCEPTED      Projected tuple-shape validation is distinguished from\n                       executable scope validation.\n```\n\nNo review finding was rejected outright. P2-9 was narrowed rather than\nrejected: shared lambda-body emission may be sound later, but is not assumed\nuntil its calling convention is specified.\n\n## Sections changed\n\n- §1, architecture, invertibility, equivalence, environment, and invariants.\n- §2, executable dimension and image identity.\n- §3, named-datom lowering, tail handling, sharing, and scope validation.\n- §4, frame VM, protocol environment, and helper reuse.\n- §5, effect and equivalence boundaries.\n- §6, phases B0 through B6.\n- §7, test matrix and non-goals.\n- §7.1, stream-topology linker treatment.\n- §8, risks and owner decisions.\n- §9, protected boundaries.\n\n## Remaining owner decisions\n\nRanked by B0 impact:\n\n1. Tempid normalization and the stable identity retained by inverse encoding.\n2. Exact result, error, closure, continuation, and stream normalizer.\n3. Approval of the `:yin.debruijn.code/*` descriptor and mirrored opcode table.\n4. Whether to fix the named VM environment leak or exclude it using fresh VM\n   instances.\n5. Whether to commission B6 hash linking, including authority, SCC identity,\n   and request-stream failure policy.\n6. Whether source-to-projection lineage is ever persisted."}}
